## Introduction

Screeding needs fewer tools than almost any other building trade, which makes ownership of the right ones non-negotiable. A straight edge rules the wall's honesty, a hawk and trowel set the surface, and a cheap spirit level that lies by 3mm ruins a week of work one bubble at a time.

This guide covers the screeding toolkit: what each tool does, the quality that matters when buying (and where budget versions are fine), maintenance that makes tools last for years, and the improvised items Nigerian screeders press into service — along with which shortcuts are safe and which show up on the finished wall.

## The Core Kit — Eight Tools, One Wall

**The straightedge (rules and staffs).** The trade's honesty instrument — 1.5–2.4 m of true, straight wood or aluminium. It rules off between screed dots, cuts the high spots, and *arbitrates flatness* at acceptance ([the quality standards](/learn/finishing-quality-standards-what-to-look-for) define what it should find). Quality that matters: genuinely straight, because a bowed edge makes every wall into the bow's image. This is the last tool to buy cheap and the first to check (sight down its length monthly).

**The spirit level.** The plumb-and-level counterpart — 600–1200 mm, used with the straightedge to set staffs and check the wall's vertical life. A level that lies (bubble off-zero at true-level) transfers its lie to every staff and every wall; test any level before trusting it (the flip test: same reading when reversed end-to-end).

**The hawk.** The hand-held board that feeds the trowel — 300–350 mm square, with its centre handle. Quality that matters: balance and a face that holds the batch without sucking its water (aluminium or a sealed plywood). The hawk is where a day's rhythm lives; a heavy or wobbly hawk taxes every single scoop.

**The trowels.** The trade's instruments in plural: the **wooden float** for spreading and closing the base coat (its slight tooth keys the surface), the **steel float (trowel)** for the finishing pass's tight, burnished face, and the **scarifier/comb** for keying between coats. Quality that matters on the steel: a blade that holds its spring and its edge — the burnish pass is only as good as the steel's temper. Trowel care is a ritual: clean, dry, and never the lever for anything (a trowel used to open paint tins is a pry-bar with a wall's finish on its conscience).

**The mixing gear.** Head-pans (level-gauged, per [the ratios guide](/learn/cement-sand-ratio-screeding-explained)), a shovel, and the mixer or hand-mixing floor. Budget versions are genuinely fine here — the discipline is in the *counting*, not the pan's brand.

**The brushes and wash gear.** The stiff wall brush, the wash bucket, and the misting apparatus (a spray-can, a perforated tin, or a pump sprayer) for dampening and curing. The humblest line on the list and the one behind more bond failures than any trowel ([the preparation guide](/learn/how-to-prepare-walls-for-screeding) explains why the wash is the job).

**The polythene and hessian.** Curing materials — sheeting to trap moisture, sacking to hold it on vertical faces ([the curing guide](/learn/curing-drying-times-screeded-walls) prices their absence). Cheap, replaceable, and decisive.

**The site book and tape.** Not decoration: the book dates every gate and batch ratio, and the tape sets out the staffs and verifies openings. The cheapest quality system on site (see [the quality control checklist](/learn/construction-quality-control-inspection-checklist)).

## Buying Quality Where It Counts — and Not Where It Doesn't

The kit divides cleanly into **instruments** (straightedge, level, steel trowel — where a few thousand naira separate a tool that keeps a wall true from one that quietly bends it) and **consumables** (pans, brushes, polythene, hessian — where the market's budget versions do the same job, because the discipline is in use, not manufacture).

The instrument logic is arithmetic: a straightedge that bows 4 mm over 2 m puts 4 mm of lie into every ruling pass on every wall it touches — a defect you pay for at *every* coat after the screed, forever. The consumable logic is the opposite arithmetic: an expensive pan carries exactly the same cement as a cheap one, and both outlive the habit of counting them level.

The middle category — the hawk and wooden float — earns a modest premium for comfort and longevity, not for the wall: the day's labour is long, and the tools that tax the wrist tax the work.

## Maintenance — the Habits That Make Tools Last a Decade

The screeder's tool life is decided at the end of each day, not at purchase:

- **Cement never sleeps on steel:** trowels and floats washed clean the same day — a cement skin baked onto a blade is ground off with the blade's temper or kept as a wreck. Ten minutes at close of work buys years.
- **Wood lives dry and oiled:** floats, hawks' plywood faces, and wooden straightedges stored dry, off the ground, and lightly oiled (burnt oil or the market's equivalents) — a swollen, delaminating float is a wobble transferred to the wall.
- **Levels and edges are checked, not assumed:** the flip test and the sighting pass, monthly and after every drop. Instruments that get dropped get *verified*.
- **The mixer and pans are batch-discipline items:** drum emptied, pans struck level and stacked — the morning's first batch inherits the evening's cleanliness or its chemistry.

## The Improvised Arsenal — Nigerian Site Ingenuity, Audited

Every screeder improvises; the honest audit is which improvisations are *safe* and which surface on the wall:

- **Plywood staffs and 2×2 rules** — safe and sound: straight, honest, and true if checked. An improvised straightedge that has been sighted for true is a straightedge.
- **The perforated tin watering-can for misting** — safe: it mists as well as any sprayer.
- **Brooms and sacking for curing cover** — safe: the physics is the coverage, not the catalogue.
- **The cut plywood "hawk"** — workable, if balanced and sealed; a face that drinks the batch's water changes the mix's consistency between scoop and wall.
- **The string-and-nail level line** — the honest ancestor of the spirit level, and still true when used with a plumb bob. What is *not* safe: eyeballing plumb "by experience" on staffs — the lie compounds down every course the staffs rule.
- **The improvised trowel — a piece of daka or a cut sheet** — not safe: the finishing pass's burnish needs spring steel; a floppy improvisation telegraphs its every flex into the surface.

The pattern: improvisation is legitimate for *support* gear and fatal for *instruments*. The wall forgives a homemade hawk; it publishes a homemade level.

## What the Tool Kit Says About the Quote

A final, practical use for this guide: the toolkit is visible before the work starts. A screeder whose kit includes a true straightedge, a tested level, steel that is clean, and polythene for the cure is quoting a *system*; a crew whose kit is a bucket, a guess, and a broom is quoting a *hope* — and the per-m² rates will differ by less than the redo will. When comparing quotes ([the costs guide](/learn/how-to-estimate-screeding-costs-nigeria)), spend the ten minutes looking at the tools: the trade is small enough that the kit tells you most of what the work will.


## The Buying Trip — a Market Walk in Order

How the kit is actually bought, in the order the market rewards: **start at the instrument stalls** (the straightedge and level) — sighting every straightedge down its length and flip-testing every level before the price talk starts, because a bowed edge discounted is still a bowed edge. **Then the steel counter:** hold the trowel, feel the spring, and reject the floppy — the finishing pass is bought at the counter or lost on the wall. **Then the support gear anywhere it is cheap:** pans, brushes, polythene, hessian, buckets — the commodity end of the market, where the haggling energy is better spent than at the instrument stall. And finish with the two purchases most trips forget: **the misting apparatus** (the tool behind the whole cure) and **the site book and tape** (the tools behind every gate this kit is used to pass).

The budget shape that survives the market: the instruments take half the budget, the steel takes a quarter, and everything else shares the rest. A kit bought in the reverse proportion — expensive pans under a bowed straightedge — is the market's most common bad purchase, because it optimizes the tools that matter least.

## The Kit and the Crew — Who Owns What

On Nigerian sites the kit's ownership decides its care. The screeder's own kit (the personal trowel and hawk, usually) is maintained like an instrument — because it is one. The site's kit (edges, levels, pans, polythene) is maintained like the schedule: only if the site has a *someone* whose job includes it. The honest arrangements: **owned by the screeder** (best care, priced into the rate), **owned by the site with a named keeper** (fine, when the keeper exists), or **owned by nobody** (the pans crack, the edge warps in the rain, and the wall publishes the neglect by the second coat). When engaging a screeder ([the costs guide](/learn/how-to-estimate-screeding-costs-nigeria)), the ownership question is one of the ten-minute quotes: a screeder who owns and maintains their instruments arrives with the work already half-supervised.

## Closing

The screeding kit is small by design — a true straightedge, an honest level, a balanced hawk, spring in the steel, counting in the pans, water in the bucket, polythene on the roll, and a book with dates in it. Buy the instruments properly, maintain them daily, improvise the support gear freely and the instruments never (a rule worth repeating, because it is the one sentence buyers forget under the market's bargaining pressure) — and the toolkit, like the wall it builds, will be telling the truth for years. Start the kit small, honest, and complete — one true edge beats three bowed ones, one clean steel float beats a drawer of neglected blades, and a polythene square costs less than any single wall it will ever speak for.
